## Introduction

The landscape of autism research has shifted considerably over the past decade, driven in part by advocacy from autistic people themselves. <cite Pellicano2014> documented a striking divergence between researcher priorities and the lived concerns of autistic adults and their families — a finding that prompted a broader conversation about whose questions research should answer.

Prevalence estimates have also been revised upward and the male-to-female ratio has been scrutinized more carefully. The review by <cite Loomes2017> found that earlier estimates of a 4:1 ratio significantly overstated the male skew, with implications for how diagnostic criteria are applied to women and girls.

## Burnout and Well-Being

Autistic burnout — a state of chronic exhaustion and reduced functioning resulting from sustained masking and sensory overload — has received growing attention in the research literature. <cite Mantzalas2022> proposed a conceptual model of the risk and protective factors that contribute to burnout, drawing on both empirical work and community testimony.

Understanding the autistic experience requires moving beyond deficit-focused frameworks. <cite CaldwellHarris2023> argues for an approach grounded in insider accounts, noting that autistic self-report reveals cognitive and social patterns that purely behavioral observation misses.

## Education and Stigma Reduction

Reducing stigma in higher education settings has been identified as a key priority. <cite GillespieLynch2017> evaluated an online training program for college students and found meaningful improvements in both knowledge and attitudes toward autistic peers.

Multi-pronged approaches combining awareness training with structural accommodation have shown the most promise. <cite Mantzalas2022, Pellicano2014> both point to the importance of community involvement in designing interventions rather than imposing them from outside.

